## 4. Animation: frame-by-frame plan
Right-handed overhand spear throw, based on real javelin technique: a long arm drawn back, a braced front leg, hips turn before shoulders, the arm comes over the top of the shoulder, then follow-through. No root motion; the character stays in place.

| Frame | Name | Hips | Torso / chest | Right arm (throwing) | Left arm | Legs and weight |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| F0 | Ready | Square to the target | Upright | Relaxed at side, elbow 20° | Relaxed, elbow 20° | Feet shoulder-width, knees 10°, weight centred |
| F4 | Summon ("Summon" marker) | Square | Upright | Hand raised to chest height, palm up, elbow 90° | Same as F0 | Same as F0 |
| F8 | Spear formed | Turn 20° away from target | Turn 30° away | Hand at shoulder height, gripping the spear horizontally | Starts rising forward | Weight starts shifting back |
| F12 | Wind-up peak | Turned 40° away | Turned 60° away (side-on): hips and shoulders 20° apart | Pulled LONG and straight behind, hand at shoulder height, elbow 10–15°, spear tip at eye level pointing at the target | Pointing straight at the target at shoulder height | Back (right) knee 30°, weight 70% on back leg; front (left) foot stepping forward |
| F13 | Hold | Hips START turning toward target (+15°) | Stays back (stretch) | Stays back | Stays | Front foot plants; front knee nearly straight (≤10°) as a block |
| F14 | Whip | Hips square to target | Shoulders start turning; torso tilts 15° to the LEFT, away from the throwing arm | Elbow comes forward HIGH, above shoulder height | Pulls down toward left hip | Weight moves onto the front leg |
| F15 | Release ("Release" marker) | 20° past square | Shoulders square, chest pushed forward | Arm fully extended forward and up about 30° above horizontal, over the top of the shoulder, hand open | At left hip | Front leg braced straight; back heel lifted |
| F18 | Follow-through | 30° past square | Leaning 30° forward | Arm sweeps down and across to the left knee | Behind the hip | Back leg swings forward, toes dragging |
| F24 | Settle | Back toward square | Leaning 10° forward | Coming up to guard | Coming up to guard | Back foot lands; knees 15° |
| F30 | Ready | Same as F0 | Same as F0 | Same as F0 | Same as F0 | Same as F0 (must match F0 within 1°) |

### Animation rules
- **Rig:** use the REAL R15 rig from my game. Read every Motor6D (Part0, Part1, C0, C1) and every part's size from Studio with run_code, then build or match the Blender armature to those numbers. Convert Roblox Y-up to Blender Z-up. Never rename, add or remove bones.
- **Order of work:**
  1. BLOCKING: the key frames above only, with CONSTANT (stepped) interpolation. Screenshot every key pose from the SIDE and 3/4 FRONT, next to the matching reference panel. Fix the poses before going further.
  2. SPLINING: switch to Bezier. F12→F15 must be FAST (anticipation into the throw). F15 onward eases out slowly.
  3. POLISH in the Graph Editor: hips lead, torso follows 1 frame later, then the shoulder, then the arm 1 frame after that (the whip). Add a small overshoot on F15 and let it settle.
- **The spear in the hand:** the spear is a VFX object welded to the RightHand. It is NOT part of the rig. In Blender, parent a 6-stud stand-in spear to the hand bone so you can check the spear's angle in every pose. The spear must point at the target from F12 to F15. Never let it go through the head or body.
- **Script checks (print the numbers each time):**
  - Elbows and knees bend 0–150°, only in their natural direction.
  - Wrists stay within 60° of the forearm direction.
  - A planted foot drifts no more than 0.05 studs.
  - Root drift is 0.
  - F30 matches F0 within 1°.
  - Turn on Blender Motion Paths for the right hand, F8–F24. The path must be one smooth arc that goes OVER the shoulder; screenshot it.
  - The release frame is the frame where the hand moves fastest forward. Report it, and check it matches the Release marker (F15).
- **Proof:** key pose screenshots next to the reference panels; an animated GIF rendered from Blender (side view, 30 fps, plus a 0.25× version); the motion path screenshot; the check results.
- **Into Roblox:** build a KeyframeSequence in Studio with run_code by sampling EVERY frame and converting each pose to Motor6D transforms relative to rest. Add KeyframeMarkers "Summon" and "Release". Test with KeyframeSequenceProvider:RegisterKeyframeSequence. Then screenshot F0, F12, F15 and F18 in Studio next to the same Blender frames. If any limb is more than about 5° off, the conversion is wrong; fix the conversion, not the poses. Publish by uploading the .rbxm through Open Cloud (assetType Animation). If Roblox refuses, save the sequence to AnimSaves and give me the one-click step. Put the asset ID in CONFIG.

